 you  really look at the challenge squarely it  is just not at the scale that we need to  be acting and it it seems to me that  most of the international negotiations  seemed to be treating the issue of the  environment as though it's just sort of  yet another issue that they have to  negotiate about and I really I feel very  strongly that the focus on trying to  stop at two degrees of climate change is  actually seriously uninformed and  seriously misguided that is a world  without tropical coral reefs and one  twelfth of humanity depends on tropical  coral reefs for their well-being and I'm  not going to sit here and add to  building the case for why two degrees is  too much but I think we all collectively  need to be working on that building more  evidence and basically Preston  those who engage in these kinds of  discussions and agreements to face the  reality that the biology of our planet  is not going to be well served by  letting climate change go to two degrees  and then of course if we really want to  stop at two degrees global emissions  have to peek in 2016 so is it all over I  know it isn't all over especially if you  look at the significant portion of  excess carbon dioxide in the atmosphere  that actually comes from three centuries  of destruction and degradation of  ecosystems worldwide and there's  absolutely no reason why a good chunk of  that maybe 50 parts per million of co2  can't be brought out back out of the  atmosphere by taking on ecosystem  restoration at a planetary scale so  having made that point I just want to  add that in to the way we think about  spatial planning that we have to  actually think about managing our planet  as the linked biological and physical  system that it is and is literally half  a degree of potential global warming  that could be avoided by doing that so  all right as we think about all these  things that actually can play in a  positive way to some of the biodiversity  conservation we were considering in the  end the two earlier talks so on a more  hopeful note about international  negotiations the Convention on  Biological Diversity having spent in my  view almost too much time  on access and benefit sharing and bio  safety protocols is now I think in a  much more serious frame of mind both as  a set of governments and as a  secretariat so whereas in 2010 at the  10th conference of the parties in Nagoya  every single nation had to admit that it  had failed to meet the biodiversity  conservation goals that they had sat  they have a whole new set of targets set  about the same time and I Ichi and  they're just meeting this past week and  the week before in India to really  seriously consider how they're actually  going to make those targets the  Secretariat is now led by a Brazilian  Valley ods who's bound and determined  that he's going to make a huge  difference through the convention on  biological diversity and the big issue  is more than anything else is going to  be the necessary financial resources to  do it a hard thing to raise in a world  with the economic conditions that we  currently have but really cheap  inexpensive I in the overall scheme of  things so that's something to both keep  an eye on and look for ways that we all  can sort of promote so what thrills me  about this particular symposium is this  emphasis on a global scale taking on the  issue at the scale it deserves and there  Watson bits and pieces that are sort of  trending in this direction around the  world particularly in civil society  in academia and I just think we all need  to wake up every morning remembering  that the problem is as big as it is and  we should be pushing for as big a scale  response as society is capable of so  that's what I really wanted to say about  global scale of things let's just always  remember how big the problem is and wake  up every morning newly fired up to do  something about that so the other part  of what I wanted to talk about and I  probably won't use my whole a lot of  time is how important this spatial  biodiversity science really is because  what we if we are if we were to succeed  and minimizing the loss of Biological  Diversity on the planet at the same time  as we deal with the increasing human  numbers and resource needs that John  displayed in such a simple and  straightforward graphic we're going to  have to have serious integrated  development planning in large units of  landscape so I'm not going to talk about  the Amazon which I usually talk about it  needs that very much but I'm thinking  I'm thinking about the savanna regions  of Africa which are now down to about  thirty percent of their original extent  and all the glorious biodiversity of  African savannas that we all rejoice in  you know is down in many cases it close  to ten percent of its original  population levels and that's a continent  with rapidly growing population  rapidly growing needs in terms of food  and which is also being looked to buy  other parts of the world as a potential  future source of food and it is also a  place where they're all these mining and  other natural resource interests that  are coming in from the outside and you  get a picture that looks like on the map  we saw of deforestation and logging  roads in the Congo so I think we have to  take the knowledge we already have which  you can see is considerable and work  that in to development planning at large  regional scales so the other supposed  and the real needs of society get met  but in a way that has far fewer  downsides for Biological Diversity and  you know you can make the same case over  again for say the mekong river and the  dams that people are thinking of putting  on the mekong river which would have a  huge impact on freshwater biodiversity  would hit the the fisheries of Cambodia  and ultimately the rice basket of  Vietnam so all these things really need  to be put together in in regional  planning something that in the end may  create more opportunity th